WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.580
LARRY ARNN: Vital
causes are the things

00:00:02.580 --> 00:00:04.110
that we seek above all others.

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:05.370
The things we would die for.

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:08.870
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:15.370 --> 00:00:17.260
If you read the Declaration
of Independence,

00:00:17.260 --> 00:00:19.690
you will find that it
comes in three parts,

00:00:19.690 --> 00:00:22.720
and in the first part it states
some universal principles.

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:24.500
Very beautiful.

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:26.620
And in the second part,
it contains a bill

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:30.250
of particulars in 17
paragraphs about bad stuff

00:00:30.250 --> 00:00:34.540
the King of England did,
which bad stuff justifies

00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:37.990
the making of America,
throwing off the old government

00:00:37.990 --> 00:00:40.570
and adopting a new one.

00:00:40.570 --> 00:00:44.240
And those things are remarkably
like the Constitution United

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:44.740
States.

00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:46.280
What did the King do wrong?

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:50.360
Well, he interfered
with the legislature,

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:53.200
which is a violation of the
first step in all government,

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:56.170
the making of laws, but also
a violation of separation

00:00:56.170 --> 00:00:58.630
of powers, which
is crucial to how

00:00:58.630 --> 00:01:00.910
the Constitution of the
United States worked.

00:01:00.910 --> 00:01:03.470
So separation of
powers is important,

00:01:03.470 --> 00:01:06.760
and then he interfered
with the people's ability

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:11.740
to elect legislators, in
other words, representation,

00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:13.900
a key feature of the
Constitution of the United

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:14.920
States.

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:17.830
And then he interfered
with the judges.

00:01:17.830 --> 00:01:21.280
He would ship people.

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:23.470
Arrest them for crimes and
ship them off to England

00:01:23.470 --> 00:01:26.200
where they couldn't be tried
by a jury of their peers

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:29.110
nor before judges
who are independent

00:01:29.110 --> 00:01:32.830
of the executive branch, which
more or less the King was.

00:01:32.830 --> 00:01:36.670
So you see, they're writing
the Constitution right there.

00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:39.890
If you have a government
that does these things,

00:01:39.890 --> 00:01:43.030
then you are justified to
rebel against that government

00:01:43.030 --> 00:01:46.930
and to kill anybody
who resists you.

00:01:46.930 --> 00:01:50.000
So it implies the Constitution.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:53.900
Now, they are different the
Declaration of Independence

00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:57.350
and the Constitution, they
serve different functions.

00:01:57.350 --> 00:02:01.093
They also sound different
in a very interesting way.

00:02:01.093 --> 00:02:03.260
The Declaration of Independence
is really beautiful.

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:07.990
"When in the course
of human events,

00:02:07.990 --> 00:02:09.577
it becomes necessary
for one people

00:02:09.577 --> 00:02:11.910
to dissolve the political
bands that have connected them

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:14.940
with another, and to assume
among the powers of the Earth

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:18.600
the separate and equal station
to which the laws of nature

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:20.160
and nature's God entitle them."

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:22.170
Isn't that a grand
expression the laws

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:25.020
of nature and of nature's God?

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:26.940
It's written like that.

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:30.030
Also, it's not time bound.

00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:32.130
When in the course
of human events,

00:02:32.130 --> 00:02:34.960
that means anytime in the
course of human events,

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:37.560
it becomes necessary for
one people, that means

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:39.870
any people, anywhere, any time.

00:02:39.870 --> 00:02:43.740
The claim is what's going to
follow in this document is

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:47.577
right at all times.

00:02:47.577 --> 00:02:49.410
And so one way to think
about the difference

00:02:49.410 --> 00:02:51.202
between the Declaration
of the Constitution

00:02:51.202 --> 00:02:56.490
is it's a document about
ultimate purposes, what

00:02:56.490 --> 00:03:00.510
they call in classic
philosophy final causes.

00:03:00.510 --> 00:03:04.350
Final causes are the things
that we seek above all others.

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:06.390
The things we would die for.

00:03:06.390 --> 00:03:09.790
Well, the Constitution
is not quite like that.

00:03:09.790 --> 00:03:15.450
The Constitution-- it
provides a form of government.

00:03:15.450 --> 00:03:18.620
And when you read it, I've
regarded it as beautiful

00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:20.660
and think I understand
why it's beautiful.

00:03:20.660 --> 00:03:22.430
It has to do with
its structure which

00:03:22.430 --> 00:03:25.610
Madison says is the most
important thing about it.

00:03:25.610 --> 00:03:34.340
But on any given passage,
it's full of details.

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:36.230
There's a list in
Article I, Section

00:03:36.230 --> 00:03:39.410
8, things the Congress can do.

00:03:39.410 --> 00:03:43.400
Well, post offices and
post roads is among them.

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:46.370
It's hard to write a
beautiful sentence about that.

00:03:46.370 --> 00:03:50.240
No, this is a document about how
the government's going to work,

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:53.720
even how it's going to look.

00:03:53.720 --> 00:03:58.280
It looks like a thing that
operates in three branches.

00:03:58.280 --> 00:04:02.000
If there's interviews going on
in front of the White House,

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:04.010
which there are every
day on the nightly news,

00:04:04.010 --> 00:04:06.260
there's a picture of the
White House in the background

00:04:06.260 --> 00:04:07.927
because that's where
the president works

00:04:07.927 --> 00:04:10.770
and that's where
executive things are done.

00:04:10.770 --> 00:04:14.120
And if there's something
big in the Congress,

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:17.800
then there's cameras looking
up at the Capitol dome

00:04:17.800 --> 00:04:19.550
because that's where
the Congress operates

00:04:19.550 --> 00:04:23.060
and where the legislation is and
the same for the Supreme Court

00:04:23.060 --> 00:04:24.350
building.

00:04:24.350 --> 00:04:28.760
In operation, the government
looks like the Constitution,

00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:32.390
and so it's become
a very great country

00:04:32.390 --> 00:04:34.700
in the course of its history.

00:04:34.700 --> 00:04:38.040
Beyond the imaginings
even of the people

00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:41.700
who founded it who were
extremely ambitious people.

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:45.420
They had the highest
possible hopes.

00:04:45.420 --> 00:04:49.560
Well, if that's true, there
must have been some cause of it,

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:52.470
but also it's in
that final purpose

00:04:52.470 --> 00:04:55.380
we serve that we have
adopted its purpose

00:04:55.380 --> 00:04:57.780
available to all human beings.

00:04:57.780 --> 00:05:01.737
We have adopted it for our own.

00:05:01.737 --> 00:05:03.070
